<p>Thirty-six countries and the European Union said they would join a planned Special Tribunal for the Crime of Aggression against Ukraine after foreign ministers in Chisinau approved a resolution linked to its creation. The supporters include 34 Council of Europe member states, Australia and Costa Rica; the Council of Europe said the move establishes an Enlarged Partial Agreement and a steering committee for the future court. The tribunal is intended to prosecute the crime of aggression arising from Russia’s February 2022 invasion of Ukraine, which officials say is not fully covered by the International Criminal Court’s current Ukraine mandate. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky signed an accord with the Council of Europe in 2025 to create the legal body, while reports said the Netherlands has carried out preparatory work to host the court in The Hague.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/nMbNqBr7eO7pJzDXzbVSweb_1h0An1pqfpCrBCm38ZvWUQvrWyDuKlA6pa08nLpjRPoN0d3_Na5u6k8vQcjlygvDg1BURgtco9ixKYobW0ankZhFTcWtbYRJ8NtoJpysJIxzujT6yT3bbAp5Yy2RVFVLc28UEfeh_X9xqsVomUjNwshsqfgF0r8hdJlhlI0c4UsVmM9EbZC9x411FjrY7FzmT__vNkfqX2iBlLhRBO4fanegQScH1ucn7tO2w91mXMzT' alt='The Council of Europe approved the basis for a future special tribunal to judge Russia’s aggression against Ukraine.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Membership gap: Twelve Council of Europe members had not joined the tribunal agreement. Reports named Hungary, Slovakia, Bulgaria and Malta among EU states absent from the list.</li><li>Operational design: Bulgarian reporting said the management committee would handle internal rules and the selection of judges and prosecutors.</li><li>Enforcement limits: DW reported that it remains unclear how the planned tribunal would force Russian defendants to appear for trial.</li><li>Initial EU money: Slovak public broadcaster STVR reported that the European Union provided the first €10 million for the tribunal’s operations in January 2026.</li></ul><blockquote>The moral 